(text "FRONTEND MATING (TOP FACE)" (exclude_from_sim no) (at 35 37 0) (effects (font (size 2.5 2.5) (bold yes)) (justify left bottom)) (uuid "6faf8a8f-3bf8-596e-a75f-14a701d99d06"))
(text_box "1. Function: Mate the Artix Frontend bottom-side receptacles J1/J2 (DF40C-70DS) with two DF40C-70DP plugs on this board's top face, 1.5 mm stack, pin n to pin n. J1 runs along the left long edge and J2 along the right long edge, parallel, pin 1 at the ASIC end. Carries the 120 ASIC signals (J1 60, J2 60), VIN_3V3 (J2.68-70, three 0.3 A contacts), VIN_5V (J1.32, one contact, an unused provision), 15 GND contacts and, from V43, the second bottom-board latch BOARD3_SPI_LATCH on J1.36 (the former spare).\n\n2. Source / changes: Claude design, pin map V6 (12 Nov 2026; 2026-11-12_FPGA_Work/09_Pin_Map_V6/PIN_MAP_V6.csv and ASIC_PORT_PINS_V6.csv; frozen 13 Nov 2026), built on pin map V5 (layout V11 lane plan; Howard, 10 Nov 2026: one Frontend connector on each long edge, every ASIC group whole on one connector). Changed from schematic V30 (map V5): 12 contacts that were spare now carry top-chip data and READ lines (J1.29, J1.31, J1.33, J1.35, J1.37, J1.39, J1.43, J1.45, J1.47, J1.49, J2.40, J2.42); 22 contacts keep their wire but carry another signal (top-chip SPI data, the top chips' own SPI clock and latch, top-chip inputs moved along the bus); J1.32 = VIN_5V; J1.30 = GND, so both row neighbours of the 5 V contact (J1.30, J1.34) are ground; J1.36 stays open here. Reason: meeting with the ASIC designer, 12 Nov 2026 (2026-11-12_FPGA_Work/07_Meeting_With_ASIC_Designer_V1/MEETING_NOTES_V1.md), answers A1 and A2 (pin order final; top chips keep their own SPI clock and latch), and Howard's decisions of 12 Nov 2026. Unchanged: VIN_3V3 on J2.68-70, GND on 14/15/27/34/41/50/66 of both. Hirose DF40 catalog, June 2024 (LCSC copy), URL https://datasheet.lcsc.com/datasheet/pdf/145ab5d527b34eafa7a47375cb561c6a.pdf?productCode=C3640153 . DF40C-70DP C2692097 / 70DS C294854 (JLC, read 2 Nov 2026). Unverified: the Frontend has not adopted map V6 yet (its next revision does); two rigid DF40 pairs between two boards (the DF40 drawing, sheet 3 item 4, advises one pair per board pair). V42 / layout V23 (Howard, 19 Nov 2026, after Zitong Xu's review point on the ground return: \"Yes, re-plan it\"): no net or pin changed. On the board, ground contacts J1.15, J1.27, J2.14 and J2.34 now have a laser via at the pad and a buried via into both GND planes (J2.41 since layout V22; J1.41 has a via pair 2.3 mm away, J2.50 a through via 1.45 mm away). J2.15 and J2.27 have none: a via there would cut the AGND strips on layer 2 that feed the local AGND vias of U3 and U10. Designed by Claude; no manufacturer reference. Layer-2 and layer-5 lanes moved for it are listed in 2026-11-19_FPGA_Work/06_FPGA_LDO_A_Layout_V23_Ground_Via_Breakout/START_HERE.md. V43 / layout V24 (Howard, 19 Nov 2026; pin map V8 (2026-11-19_FPGA_Work/10_Pin_Map_V8_One_Latch_Per_ASIC_Board/PIN_MAP_V8.csv)): J1.36 now carries BOARD3_SPI_LATCH to J3.61 and J2.48 carries BOARD1_SPI_LATCH to J4.61 only, so each ASIC board has its own SPI latch (meeting with the ASIC designer, 12 Nov 2026 (2026-11-12_FPGA_Work/07_Meeting_With_ASIC_Designer_V1/MEETING_NOTES_V1.md)). The Frontend already wires J1.36 to FPGA ball V14; no other contact changed. On the board the ground via pair of J1.34 moved (buried via 0.34 mm north, laser via 0.11 mm along the pad) so that pad 36 can take a laser via. Designed by Claude. Unverified: pin file not run in Vivado." (exclude_from_sim no) (at 30 490 0) (size 165 72) (margins 1.5 1.5 1.5 1.5) (stroke (width 0.3) (type solid)) (fill (type none)) (effects (font (size 1.5 1.5)) (justify left top)) (uuid "500f833a-10bd-52e3-a402-0254b0231284"))
